<article class="env-warning" id="04VF">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04VF">Warning <span data-tag="04VF">4.7.5.7</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of Kan complexes. The assertion that $f$ is a monomorphism can be given two different interpretations: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The map $f$ is a monomorphism in the ordinary category of $\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ of simplicial sets. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The map $f$ is a monomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ of spaces. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Beware that these conditions are unrelated to one another. Condition $(b)$ is homotopy invariant: it is the requirement that $f$ restricts to a homotopy equivalence of $X$ with a summand of $Y$ (Example <a data-tag="05FW" href="/tag/05FW">4.7.5.6</a>). Condition $(a)$ is very far from being homotopy invariant: we can always arrange that it is satisfied after replacing $Y$ by a homotopy equivalent Kan complex (see Exercise <a data-tag="00UY" href="/tag/00UY">3.1.8.11</a>). </p>
</article>
